# E23b -- Fire a real cavern invasion and see whether v5.8's exclusion actually holds. Addendum 49 shipped the rule that DF's own invaders — invasion_id >= 0 — leave every layer count, because one raid can make a cavern look stocked and silence the job that keeps it alive. That rule has never met an invasion: none has fired in any run to date, so the exclusion has only ever been exercised against zero invaders. DF's shipped agitation-rebalance shows where the roll lives — each cavern's feature_init_subterranean_from_layerst carries feature.irritation_level, worth irritation/10000 of attack chance per season, capped at 100,000 — so one arm pins all three cavern features at 100,000 — re-pinned at every sample, after the count is taken, so DF cannot quietly decay the condition out from under the arm the way it decays outdoor irritation — and runs two seasons to guarantee a seasonal roll; the control leaves them where DF has them. The tool runs in both arms with the cavern layer managed and no ceiling, so the counts under test are the shipped ones. Two replicates each.
run 20260918-210056 fort CTRL backup CTRL.preverify
DF v0.53.16 win64 STEAM DFHack 53.16-r1.1 world The Legendary Dimension site 32 tiles 29..29,20..20 seed WILDERPOP1
commits: DwarfCron 835fddb seasonal-wildlife 41bbd19 fps cap 1000

## Predictions (written before the run)
- the exclusion works: invaders appear on the map in the irritated arm, invaders>0 is logged, and the cavern count never counts them — cavern tracks the control while invaders climb
- the exclusion is a silent no-op: invaders appear and the cavern count rises with them, which means WILD.field resolved to false or to the wrong name on this build
- irritation alone does not fire an invasion: no unit ever carries invasion_id >= 0 despite both cavern features pinned at maximum for two seasons, which means the roll needs something else — a civ with a route, or wealth — and v6.1's cavern-raid module cannot be tested on this fort

